# NOTES #

[LINK: (^) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_Folio#cite_ref-1]  More generally, the term first folio is employed in other appropriate contexts, as in connection with the  [LINK: (first folio collection of Ben Jonson's works) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ben_Jonson_folios]  (1616), or the first folio collection of the plays in the  [LINK: (Beaumont and Fletcher canon) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beaumont_and_Fletcher_folios]  (1647).

[LINK: (^) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_Folio#cite_ref-9]  The exact years of his London career are unknown, but biographers suggest that it may have begun any time from the mid-1580s to just before  [LINK: (Robert Greene) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_Greene_(dramatist)]  mentions Shakespeare in his  [LINK: (Groats-Worth of Wit) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greene%27s_Groats-Worth_of_Wit] . [LINK: ([5]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_Folio#cite_note-FOOTNOTEWells200628-6]  [LINK: ([6]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_Folio#cite_note-FOOTNOTESchoenbaum1987144–146-7]  [LINK: ([7]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_Folio#cite_note-FOOTNOTEChambers193059-8]

[LINK: (^) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_Folio#cite_ref-11]  Dates follow the  [LINK: (Julian calendar) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Julian_calendar] , used in England throughout Shakespeare's lifespan, but with the start of the year adjusted to 1 January (see  [LINK: (Old Style and New Style dates) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Old_Style_and_New_Style_dates] ). Under the  [LINK: (Gregorian calendar) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gregorian_calendar] , adopted in Catholic countries in 1582, Shakespeare died on 3 May. [LINK: ([8]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_Folio#cite_note-FOOTNOTESchoenbaum1987xv-10]

[LINK: (^) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_Folio#cite_ref-19]   [LINK: (Edward Fairfax) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edward_Fairfax] 's translation of  [LINK: (Torquato Tasso) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Torquato_Tasso] 's  [LINK: (Godfrey of Bulloigne) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jerusalem_Delivered]  (1600),  [LINK: (Thomas Heywood) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Heywood] 's  [LINK: (Troia Britanica) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Troia_Britanica&action=edit&redlink=1]  (1609), and  [LINK: (Boccaccio) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boccaccio] 's  [LINK: (Decameron) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Decameron]  (1620) was published in folio by  [LINK: (William) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_Jaggard]  and Isaac Jaggard;  [LINK: (Montaigne) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Montaigne] 's  [LINK: (Essays) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Essays_(Montaigne)]  (1603, 1613),  [LINK: (Samuel Daniel) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Samuel_Daniel] 's  [LINK: (Panegyricke Congratulatory) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Panegyricke_Congratulatory&action=edit&redlink=1]  (1603),  [LINK: (Lucan) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lucan] 's  [LINK: (Pharsalia) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pharsalia]  (1614), and  [LINK: (James Mabbe) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_Mabbe] 's translation of  [LINK: (Mateo Alemán) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mateo_Alem%C3%A1n] 's  [LINK: (The Rogue) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guzm%C3%A1n_de_Alfarache]  (1623) were published by  [LINK: (Edward Blount) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edward_Blount] ; and  [LINK: (John Smethwick) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Smethwick]  published  [LINK: (Ben Jonson) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ben_Jonson] 's  [LINK: (Works) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ben_Jonson_folios]  (1616), and  [LINK: (Michael Drayton) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Michael_Drayton] 's  [LINK: (Poems) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Poems_(Drayton)&action=edit&redlink=1]  (1619). All told, a quarter of the literary folios produced in London between 1600 and 1623 were the work of these three publishers. [LINK: ([15]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_Folio#cite_note-FOOTNOTERasmussen201626-18]

[LINK: (^) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_Folio#cite_ref-35]  He also cites previous estimates from Anthony James West, based in part on unpublished estimates by Peter Blayney, that the publisher's cost was about 6s 8 [LINK: (d) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Penny_(English_coin)]  (equivalent to £77 in 2023), and the wholesale price no more than 10s (equivalent to £116 in 2023). [LINK: ([30]) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_Folio#cite_note-FOOTNOTEMayer2016105-34]


