[LINK: (Hinman, Charlton) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charlton_Hinman]  (1963). The Printing and Proof-Reading of the First Folio. Oxford:  [LINK: (Clarendon Press) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clarendon_Press] .  [LINK: (hdl) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hdl_(identifier)] :2027/mdp.39015002186073.  [LINK: (ISBN) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ISBN_(identifier)]   [LINK: (978-0198116134) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:BookSources/978-0198116134] .  [LINK: (OCLC) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/OCLC_(identifier)]  164878968. {{ [LINK: (cite book) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Template:Cite_book] }}: ISBN / Date incompatibility ( [LINK: (help) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help:CS1_errors#invalid_isbn_date] )

[LINK: (Nicholson, Catherine) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Catherine_Nicholson&action=edit&redlink=1] , "Theater for a New Audience" (review of  [LINK: (Emma Smith) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emma_Smith] , Shakespeare's First Folio: Four Centuries of an Iconic Book,  [LINK: (Oxford University Press) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oxford_University_Press] , 2023, 379 pp.;  [LINK: (Chris Laoutaris) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Chris_Laoutaris&action=edit&redlink=1] , Shakespeare's Book: The Story Behind the First Folio and the Making of Shakespeare, Pegasus, 2023, 516 pp.; and Ben Higgins, Shakespeare's Syndicate: The First Folio, Its Publishers, and the Early Modern Book Trade,  [LINK: (Oxford University Press) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oxford_University_Press] , 2023, 291 pp.),  [LINK: (The New York Review of Books) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_New_York_Review_of_Books] , vol. LXX, no. 17 (2 November 2023), pp. 57–59. " [LINK: (Emma Smith) -> https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emma_Smith]  argues in Shakespeare's First Folio: Four Centuries of an Iconic Book, first published in 2016 and now reissued with a fresh preface, [that] it is possible to overstate both the value and the significance of the 1623 Shakespeare folio. Indeed, Smith suggests, that is what we continually do, exaggerating its rarity, inflating its price, overlooking its textual and bibliographic weaknesses, fantasizing about its relation to Shakespeare himself, and forgetting or willfully obscuring the million accidents and historical contingencies that have facilitated its rise." (p. 57.)

